+    <section class="journal" id="roasters-journal">
+      <h2>Roaster's Journal</h2>
+      <div class="journal-text" id="journal-text">
+        <p>Every bag we roast begins months before the beans reach our shop, usually with a plane ticket and a long
+          dirt road. Each year our head roaster travels to the highlands of Colombia, Ethiopia, and Guatemala to walk
+          the farms we buy from. We taste fresh crops at the cupping tables of small cooperatives, meet the families who
+          pick and process the cherries, and talk honestly about what the season looked like: rainfall, harvest timing,
+          and the problems no spreadsheet will tell you about.</p>
+        <p>We buy directly from producers wherever we can, and we pay well above the Fair Trade minimum. Prices are
+          agreed before harvest, so farmers can plan their year and invest in drying beds, shade trees, and better
+          wet-mill equipment. When a lot is exceptional, we pay a further quality premium, and we share the cupping
+          scores with the farm so they know exactly what earned it. Many of our relationships are now six or seven years
+          old, and that continuity shows up in the cup.</p>
+        <p>Once a sample passes our table, we commit to a small lot and arrange shipping in sealed, grain-lined bags to
+          protect freshness. Green coffee rests for a few weeks after arrival before we profile-roast it in small
+          batches. We cup every batch against the reference sample, and anything that misses the mark is set aside
+          rather than sold. Our goal is simple: a cup you can trace back to a place, a person, and a harvest, roasted
+          within days of landing in your hands.</p>
+      </div>
+
+      <button type="button" class="btn btn-summarize" id="summarize-btn">Summarize</button>
+      <div class="summary" id="summary" role="status" aria-live="polite" hidden></div>
+    </section>
   </main>
 
